e most part  that's what the students are choosing  from and there's a huge range of project  so things that don't involve any  programming at all like adding to our  word list and finding obscure languages  and adding words in or even tracing the  history of math textbooks or things like  generating art using neural networks but  then there are really programming having  projects like connecting to d-wave API  or loris appear connected ro s to the  Wolfram language and was able to that  drone is being powered by his code  another project is fingerprint  identification and then there are things  that are sort of in between like medium  programming so building classifiers or  can't really tell it's a bad image but  that codes part of a cryptography pod  project so with that I'm going to  introduce Emma and let her take things  but i'll be around after to answer  questions if you have any about the  mentorship program ok so I'm in eighth  grader in New York and I've been in the  mentorship program since Scott January  so let me tell you how it all got  started so last year I went to a maker  faire at in New York which is like all  this like these tech foots going present  their work and dr. Wolfram was making a  talk there and I my tenant has talked  and then I was I actually discussed with  him a project i was doing involving  image identification and he pointed me  in the direction of the mentorship  program like a few months later and then  he actually also sent me his then pretty  new book an elementary introduction to  the Wolfram language and actually one  morning I was called into the  principal's office and I thought that I  had done something wrong but then it  turned out that dr. Wolfram and actually  mailed me a book and then they want to  give it to me so that was pretty cool so  I joined the mentorship program this  January and I started by learning how to  do the Wolfen language because prior to  that I didn't really  have much experience in it so I began by  following with the book mainly the  online version I worked through the  exercises which are part of each chapter  of the book which I thought were really  helpful and I met up weekly with my  mentors and we discussed all these  challenging areas that I was having  trouble with and then after I a period  of time when I got pretty comfortable  with the language I identified a project  with them so I could apply what I've  learned so what I really like about the  Wolfram language in terms of learning  experience is how easy and  instantaneously you can do  visualizations so in other language I've  learned like scratch java Swift there  was a lot of setup you have to do before  you can actually generate stuffs and  there's a lot of things that you get to  initially learn and learn how to do  before you can actually get your hands  into coding what I really like about  Mathematica and the Wolfram language is  that you can easily just generate a line  of code that will generate things like  circle states or 3d images of like of  these of structures of for example here  I have a skull I also really like how  does a lot of built-in data so in other  languages you have to kind of import  data or take data from somewhere else  and it takes a lot of time to learn how  to import that data and then process it  and then make it usable hear what I  really like is that mathematica  everything is built into the language so  you can connect to Wolfram Alpha and get  pictures of planets for example and then  I can play around with that and there's  also I can there's also a really wide  range of data I can get so we also have  things like speeches and then we have  texts from books and things like that so  there are really a lot of applications  of the language that exists that you  don't need to have a lot of experience  with the language until to get there you  can just start playing around with it so  what's different about learning the  Wolfram language with my experience s